<!-- SECTION 3: WHAT IS GSTAT APPEAL FILING -->
<section class="content-section" id="what-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="two-column">
            <div class="column-content">
                <h2 class="section-title">What Is a Trademark Hearing?</h2>
                <div class="content-text what-is-definition">
                    
                    <p>A trademark hearing (show cause hearing) is a formal proceeding at the Trademark Registry where the applicant presents oral arguments, evidence, and case law to defend their application. It occurs after the written reply to the examination report fails to satisfy the Examiner. This is the last chance before the Registrar's final decision.</p><p>Objections arise under Section 9 (absolute grounds: lack of distinctiveness, descriptiveness, genericness, deceptiveness) and Section 11 (relative grounds: similarity with earlier marks). The applicant must respond within 30 days. If the reply fails, a show cause hearing notice is issued.</p><p>For Pune applicants: TM Registry Mumbai (jurisdiction for Maharashtra). Virtual hearings available since 2020. Outcomes: accepted, accepted with conditions, or refused (appealable to Bombay HC). Related: <a href="#">Trademark Objection Reply</a>.</p>

                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:20px;">
                    <p><strong>Key Terms for Trademark Hearing in Pune:</strong></p>
                    <ul><li><strong>Show Cause Hearing:</strong> Formal oral proceeding. Applicant's last opportunity before acceptance or refusal</li><li><strong>Section 9 (Absolute):</strong> Mark lacks distinctiveness, descriptive, generic, deceptive, or against public order</li><li><strong>Section 11 (Relative):</strong> Similar/identical to earlier registered or pending mark for same/similar goods</li><li><strong>Acquired Distinctiveness:</strong> Defence that descriptive mark gained distinctiveness through commercial use</li><li><strong>TM Journal:</strong> If accepted, mark published for 4-month opposition period before registration certificate</li></ul>

                </div>
            </div>
